# Rust observability

See also: `languages/rust` (230) — base style + idioms. `foundations/localization` (050) — every
emitted string is localized; log messages target the system locale.

Applies to every library, adapter, and service crate that emits diagnostic output. CLIs and tests
have narrow exemptions noted below.

## `tracing`, not `println!`

Libraries MUST use the `tracing` crate. `println!` and `eprintln!` are acceptable only in:

- Small CLI binaries that emit deliberate operator-facing output.
- Examples and test fixtures.
- `Display` / `Debug` impls themselves (where `tracing` would be circular).

```rust
// Good — structured event, queryable in any tracing subscriber
tracing::info!(workflow_id = %plan.id(), phase = ?plan.phase, "workflow phase entered");

// Bad — unstructured stdout; invisible to Grafana, Loki, journald, or test capture
println!("workflow {} entered phase {:?}", plan.id(), plan.phase);
```

`println!` from inside a library is a workspace-level lint:

```toml
[workspace.lints.clippy]
print_stdout = "warn"
print_stderr = "warn"
dbg_macro = "deny"
```

`dbg!` is always denied — it's a development aid, not production telemetry.

## Correlation IDs on workflow/agent paths

Functions on workflow/agent/PR execution paths SHOULD carry correlation IDs via
`#[tracing::instrument]` so every emitted event can be filtered to a single run end-to-end. This
is non-negotiable for any code path the operator might need to debug from logs.

```rust
#[tracing::instrument(
    skip(executor, plan),
    fields(
        workflow_id = %plan.workflow_id(),
        run_id      = %plan.run_id(),
        phase_id    = tracing::field::Empty,  // populated when entering a phase
    ),
)]
async fn execute_plan(executor: &Executor, plan: WorkflowPlan) -> Result<ExecutionReport> {
    /* ... */
}
```

Notes:

- `skip(...)` keeps large arguments (executor handle, full plan) out of every event payload.
- `fields(...)` declares the correlation surface up front. Fields populated later use
  `tracing::field::Empty` as a placeholder, then `tracing::Span::current().record("phase_id", &%id)`
  inside the body.
- Use `%foo` for `Display` formatting, `?foo` for `Debug`. Pick the one the operator will read.

Cross-task spans: `tracing::Span::current()` captures the active span; `instrument` on the spawned
future propagates it. Tasks created without `instrument` lose the parent context — every spawn that
matters for diagnostics MUST be instrumented.

## Error source-chain preservation

Do not stringify errors at intermediate layers. Preserve the typed `source()` chain all the way to
the reporting boundary; consumers downstream (log scrapers, alerting, support runbooks) extract the
chain.

```rust
// Good — typed error, source chain preserved
#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum WorkflowError {
    #[error("policy evaluation failed for {workflow_id}")]
    PolicyEval {
        workflow_id: WorkflowId,
        #[source]
        cause: PolicyEvalError,
    },
}

// At the reporting boundary (CLI / FFI / log line):
tracing::error!(error = ?err, "workflow failed");
// `error = ?err` records the error's `Debug` representation as a field.
// If you need each `source()` level rendered, use a formatter/layer or helper that
// explicitly formats or records the source chain.

// Bad — early stringification erases the cause
return Err(WorkflowError::Generic(format!("policy eval failed: {e}")));
```

The `thiserror` `#[source]` attribute marks the wrapped error so `Error::source()` returns it.
`anyhow::Error` preserves source chains automatically — its `Debug` impl walks them.

At the actual log emission point, the structured field carries the error; never serialize the
error chain into the message text:

```rust
// Good
tracing::error!(error = ?err, workflow_id = %id, "workflow failed");

// Bad — error becomes a string in the message; cause chain is lost
tracing::error!("workflow {} failed: {:?}", id, err);
```

## Spans for long-running operations

Use `tracing::span!` (or `#[instrument]`) for any operation that lasts long enough to be visible
in latency graphs — handle of a request, evaluation of a policy pack, drainage of an event stream.
Spans give the operator a duration measurement and a parent/child tree without per-event timestamp
math.

```rust
async fn evaluate_policy_pack(pack: &PolicyPack, target: &Artifact) -> PolicyOutcome {
    let _span = tracing::info_span!("policy.evaluate", pack = %pack.id(), target = %target.id()).entered();
    /* ... evaluation ... */
}
```

The `_span` binding stays in scope for the function body. RAII drops it at function exit; the
subscriber records the duration.

## Log-message strings are localized

Per `foundations/localization` (050): every emitted string is localized through the project's
localization framework, including log messages. Log messages come from the **system catalog**
(for example `messages/system.edn`, with no operator override) so engineering tooling — Grafana
queries, Loki filters, alert templates — keeps working regardless of which operator-facing locale
is active.

Concretely: a project's `mfc-strings` (or equivalent) crate declares log-message constants under a
`logs` module; tracing call sites reference the const via the `"{}"` placeholder (`tracing` macros
require the message be a format-string literal at the macro site):

```rust
use mfc_strings as s;

tracing::warn!(
    error = %e,
    path = %p.display(),
    "{}", s::LOG_TAILER_EXIT_ON_ERROR,
);
```

The constant lives in one place; future locale additions or string audits find it via `grep -r
mfc_strings::LOG_`.

## What NOT to do

- Do not use `println!` / `eprintln!` / `dbg!` in library or service code.
- Do not stringify errors at intermediate layers — preserve `source()` chains.
- Do not skip `#[instrument]` on workflow/agent execution paths.
- Do not embed structured data inside the message string — use named fields.
- Do not emit hardcoded English log strings without going through the localization layer (see
  `foundations/localization`).
- Do not log at the wrong level — `info` is for routine events the operator wants to see, `debug`
  is for engineers, `warn` is for unexpected-but-recoverable, `error` is for things that need
  attention.

